    "questionCode": "MH-9-MTH2-1-1.2.1-OSC-101",
    "text": "Points \\(P, Q, R\\) lie on a line such that \\(d(P, Q) = 4\\), \\(d(Q, R) = 5\\), and \\(d(P, R) = 9\\). Which point lies between the other two?",
    "options": [
      {
        "text": "Point \\(P\\) (betweenness \\(Q-P-R\\))",
        "isCorrect": false
      },
      {
        "text": "Point \\(R\\) (betweenness \\(P-R-Q\\))",
        "isCorrect": false
      },
      {
        "text": "The points are non-collinear",
        "isCorrect": false
      },
      {
        "text": "Point \\(Q\\) (betweenness \\(P-Q-R\\))",
        "isCorrect": true
      }
    ],
    "correctAnswer": "D",
    "solution": "Step 1: Check the betweenness condition: if \\(d(P, Q) + d(Q, R) = d(P, R)\\), then point \\(Q\\) lies between \\(P\\) and \\(R\\).\nStep 2: Here, \\(4 + 5 = 9 = d(P, R)\\).\nStep 3: Therefore, point \\(Q\\) lies between \\(P\\) and \\(R\\), written as \\(P-Q-R\\).",
