    "questionCode": "MH-9-MTH2-2-2.1.2-OSC-101",
    "text": "To prove by indirect method that two coplanar lines \\(l\\) and \\(m\\) intersected by a transversal are parallel, what contradiction is reached?",
    "options": [
      {
        "text": "The lines would become perpendicular",
        "isCorrect": false
      },
      {
        "text": "The transversal would cease to exist",
        "isCorrect": false
      },
      {
        "text": "The angles would become zero",
        "isCorrect": false
      },
      {
        "text": "A triangle would be formed whose angle sum exceeds 180°, contradicting Euclid's postulate",
        "isCorrect": true
      }
    ],
    "correctAnswer": "D",
    "solution": "Step 1: In the indirect proof of the interior angles theorem, we assume lines \\(l\\) and \\(m\\) are not parallel, so they intersect at a point \\(P\\).\nStep 2: Then triangle \\(\\triangle ABP\\) is formed with angle sum \\(180^\\circ + \\angle P > 180^\\circ\\).\nStep 3: This contradicts the triangle angle sum theorem (sum = 180°), proving the lines must be parallel.",
